          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a reference note names a root like K21, what do I look for to get to the specific code?",
          "answer": "Based on the retrieved material, here is what to look for. I could not find the exact subcode characters in these sources, so you will need the official ICD-10-CM tabular list for those.\n\n**1. Find the branch: with or without esophagitis.** K21 has one main distinction, whether esophagitis is present (coding-gerd-k21). Reflux disease with esophagitis is one branch. Reflux disease without esophagitis is the other. The \"without\" code is the one used for most records, because the distinction rests on an endoscopic finding that many records do not contain.\n\n**2. Within the \"with esophagitis\" branch, check for bleeding.** That branch is divided further by whether bleeding is documented (coding-gerd-k21).\n\n**3. Use only documented findings.** Esophagitis is a mucosal finding. It cannot be inferred from how severe the heartburn is, from the treatment prescribed, or from how long the condition has lasted (coding-gerd-k21). So you look for a documented finding in the note, not for clues in the symptoms or medication.\n\n**4. Apply the general conventions.**\n- **Excludes1** means two conditions cannot both be present, and the codes are never reported together. **Excludes2** means both can be present and both may be reported (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions).\n- **Code first** and **use additional code** appear as a pair. They fix the order: the underlying condition is reported first, then its manifestation (same source).\n- **Combination codes** carry two conditions, or a condition and a complication, in one code. Where one exists, it is used instead of separate codes (same source).\n- The Alphabetic Index links two conditions with \"with\" and reads this as \"associated with\" or \"due to,\" presuming the link unless the record says they are unrelated. This is described in the diabetes example (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11) and the general conventions document. I did not find a passage confirming how this applies to K21 specifically, so check the index entry for \"reflux, with esophagitis.\"\n\n**5. Where the sources stop.** The retrieved passages do not list the exact subcode characters under K21, the bleeding subdivision codes, or the full Excludes notes for K20 to K31. Read those from the official ICD-10-CM tabular list. Check the codes against the note's documented findings, not against the category name alone.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
